  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1144 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nAnother chilly day is present across the Mid-South with current\ntemperatures in the 50s to lower 60s under mostly clear skies as \na 1024mb high remains parked over the Lower Mississippi Valley. \nTaking a look at the upper-levels, weak northwest flow continues \nto build into the Middle Mississippi Valley. Dry air remains over \nthe our forecast area keeping us bone-dry, meanwhile an upper- \nlevel low continues to rotate over the California Baja giving lift\nto showers and thunderstorms over southern Texas. As winds go \ncalm overnight and ample radiational cooling occurs alongside \nnarrow dewpoint depressions and clear skies, patchy fog is \nexpected, especially in low-lying river areas. Early morning \ncommuters should use caution on the roads before fog begins to \nburn off by mid-morning as mixing occurs. Some early morning frost\nmay also be witnessed mainly over areas east of the Mississippi \narea where overnight lows drop into the 30s.\n\nAs we head into the workweek, a warming trend will begin with\nreturn flow ahead of weak upper-level troughing Tuesday.\nTemperatures will begin to moderate with highs in the upper 60s\nto lower 70s. As aforementioned troughing begins to push into the\nMiddle Mississippi Valley from Southern Plains, it will begin to\nweaken and become an open wave, increasing our dewpoints, but not\nbringing enough moisture for precipitation Tuesday. Come late\nWednesday into early Thursday, a surface low and deepening trough\nwill begin pushing into the Upper-Mississippi Valley from the\nMidwest. This next system will bring ample moisture, increasing\nPWATs to around 1.1-1.2\", nearing the 90th percentile for this\ntime of the year. An upper-level jet will also begin to dip down\nover the Mid-South Wednesday ahead of this system. As these\nfeatures move in, our best shot at precipitation over the next\n7 days will exist Wednesday afternoon into evening. Due to\nuncertainty with the overall evolution of this trough and upper-\nlevel jet, shower and elevated thunderstorm chances remain 20-\n40%.\n\nAn attendant cold front will also sweep the region Thursday\nbringing much cooler air behind it. Temperatures will begin to\nplummet behind this front as cool high pressure, nearly polar in\nnature, begins to push into the region. Thursday's forecast highs\nare in the upper 50s to lower 60s with Friday's highs in the\nupper 40s to mid 50s. Though we are still several days out,\nFriday looks to be pretty brisk as the upper-level pressure\ngradient behind this deepening trough begins to tighten. For now,\nbe sure to have those coats handy late week and into the weekend.\nSaturday morning could also bring our first deep freeze of the\nseason with deterministic lows in the upper 20s to lower 30s and\nlooking probabilistically, latest LREF and NBM guidance is\nhinting at a 60-85% chance of temperatures below freezing.\n\nAEH",
